From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:08:08 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] hbbs: admit only holders of an access ticket (off by
 default)

From 1.8.0 HJViewer is for Yonsei University staff. hjv-api signs
short-lived Ed25519 tickets for staff signed in with Yonsei SSO, the PCs
bound to them, the App Review account and whoever enters the access
password. The apps only carry them: PunchHoleRequest.token,
RequestRelay.token and field 1001 of RegisterPk, which this server's
hbb_common does not declare and protobuf keeps as an unknown field. hbbs
now checks them offline, as hjviewer's docs/access-tickets.md defines the
ticket, the decisions and the file:

- A punch hole request is checked after the key and the lowest app
  version, a relay request first, both before the peer is looked up. A
  refusal is TEXT in PunchHoleResponse.other_failure or
  RelayResponse.refuse_reason, and the peer never hears of the request.
- A registration over TCP/WebSocket passes the checks of old and has its
  key stored as before, which hjv-api needs to bind a new PC. Without a
  good ticket the answer is NOT_DEPLOYED (keep_alive 30, the one refusal
  older apps take quietly), and the address, signaling channel and
  registration time of its ID stay as they were: PeerMap::store_pk is
  update_pk without those.
- An admitted registration keeps the kid, iat and exp of its ticket on
  its connection. The heartbeat closes the connection once the ticket has
  run out, NOT_BEFORE has passed it, its key is gone, or it came in while
  nothing was checked. A newer ticket registered on the same connection
  extends it.
- No registration over UDP is taken while the tickets are enforced. The
  probe of test_hbbs, which stops hbbs when it goes unanswered, still
  gets the answer of an ID nobody registered.

The rule is a file named with --hjv-access-file (HJV_ACCESS_FILE), read
again every 10 seconds; without the option hbbs does and logs what it did
before:

  MODE=enforce                   off, count or enforce
  KEY=1:<public key>             one line, two while the keys change
  NOT_BEFORE=                    UTC to the second, empty for none
  TEXT=... (access not allowed)  optional; a built-in text without it

MODE=off checks nothing, count decides and counts without refusing, and
enforce refuses. Unlike the min-controller file this one fails closed.
With the option and no usable file at the start every request is refused
(the built-in text, NOT_DEPLOYED, no UDP). A file that breaks or goes
away later leaves the last good rule in force. Either way an error says
why, again every hour. The file is read as 0005 reads its own (64 KiB,
UTF-8, every line ending in a line break), with what keeps a mistake from
doing harm:

- MODE=off reads no other value, so a mistake elsewhere never keeps the
  check from being turned off; the lines are still counted and named.
- Under count and enforce the NOT_BEFORE line must be there, even empty.
  It comes after the keys, so a file cut short at a line break is never
  read as a rule without it or with fewer keys.
- TEXT keeps 0005's rules with "access not allowed" in place of
  "version not allowed", which it must not contain: every app stops
  asking again, and 1.8.0 knows the refusal.

A ticket is checked in the contract's order: its size; its form (t1, a
kid of 1 to 65535, strict base64url); the key that kid names; the Ed25519
signature (libsodium) over "hjv-ticket-v1" NUL and the payload. Only then
is the payload read: the canonical JSON of nine members, one of six
kinds, their targets, a lifetime of 7 days at most. Then come the use for
the point, exp, iat with 60 seconds of skew, NOT_BEFORE, and the PC or
the target.

The console command hjv-access answers one line, "HJVACCESS1
mode=<off|count|enforce|closed>" and the counts since the start as
<ph|rr|reg|udp>.<reason>=<n>, with no ID or address. Closings count under
reg with the reason they came from, and a registration over UDP that is
dropped counts as udp.ignored. Refusals and closings are logged as 0005
logs its refusals, 30 lines in 10 seconds at most, with the address, the
point, the ID and the reason; no part of a ticket, no sub and no TEXT.

tests/vectors/access-tickets.json is hjviewer's
server/api/tests/vectors/access-tickets.json as of its first commit. The
unit tests read it, or the file HJV_ACCESS_VECTORS names, and pin its
SHA-256: every ticket and check comes to its reason under each mode, the
RegisterPk bytes give their field 1001, and the 39 files are read or
refused. tests/access.rs runs hbbs with tickets signed with the same test
keys at run time. Without the option, or with MODE=off, nothing changes.
Controllers without a good ticket are refused and the peer hears nothing,
also for an ID that does not exist. A relay request alone is refused. A
registration without one stays offline with its key known to hjv-pk and
cannot push an admitted PC out. Expiry, a newer ticket, a key taken out,
NOT_BEFORE and the switch from off and from count all close what they
should. A missing, broken or half-written file at the start refuses
everything until it is put right, and one that breaks later changes
nothing. UDP registrations are dropped while test_hbbs keeps hbbs
running. The key and the lowest version come first. The vectors' bytes
are read off the wire, and the refusal also goes out over plain TCP.

